  "preamble": "Diphthongization of [a] to [au]. The clearest documented case is Icelandic, where Old Norse long á /aː/ became the diphthong /au/ (Old Norse máni > Icelandic máni [ˈmauːnɪ] 'moon'), while Swedish, Norwegian and Danish rounded the same vowel to å /oː/. The Icelandic-only long ǫ́ /ɔː/ of the 11th–12th centuries merged back into /aː/ before the diphthongization, so it is not an intermediate stage of these words.",
